 <!-- Help End: Customers -->
+<!-- Help Begin: CreditControl -->
+<h2><a id="CreditControl">Credit Control</a></h2>
+
+<p>Credit control applies the same rules to every customer every time and keeps a history of what was decided, by whom and when. It uses the credit status that every customer already has, and adds credit reviews, reminder letters, month end interest and late fees, bad debt write offs and a credit control report. Credit control work needs the <b>Credit Control</b> security token (the credit controller and deputy); the Finance Director also has the <b>Credit Control - Finance Director</b> token. Both are given to roles in Setup &gt; Security Roles.</p>
+
+<h3>Normal, on watch and on stop</h3>
+
+<p>Every customer is in one of three states, set by their Credit Status (Credit Status Maintenance, under the Setup tab, says what each status means):</p>
+<ul>
+    <li><b>Normal</b> - supplied as usual.</li>
+    <li><b>On watch</b> - still supplied, but anyone taking an order for the customer is warned, and the order is listed on the Credit Control Orders screen. Customers are never told they are on watch.</li>
+    <li><b>On stop</b> - no new orders are accepted for the customer from any route: the sales office, the customer's own web logon, the web shop (the API returns a customer-on-stop error), electronic (EDI) orders or recurring orders. Orders taken before the stop cannot be dispatched or invoiced unless credit control releases them on the Credit Control Orders screen.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>The state shows on the customer inquiry and account screens and wherever an order is taken. Sales staff and sales reps can see the state and the reason but cannot change it or the credit limit.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditControlCustomer">The customer credit file</a></h3>
+
+<p>Under Maintenance, Customer Credit File (or the Credit file link on the customer inquiry) credit control can:</p>
+<ul>
+    <li><b>Record a credit review</b> - the outcome (limit confirmed, raised, reduced, put on watch, put on stop), a new limit where it is raised or reduced, notes and the next review date. The customer's limit changes as part of the review so the limit and the reason are always recorded together. Reviews cannot be changed or deleted.</li>
+    <li><b>Put the customer on stop or watch, or back to normal</b>, with a reason.</li>
+    <li><b>Mark an invoice as disputed.</b> A disputed invoice is not chased in the reminder run and is not charged interest until the dispute is marked settled.</li>
+    <li><b>Exempt the customer from interest and/or late fees</b>, and make a note where a debt has been handed to a collection agency or solicitor (those are handled outside the system).</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Every action is added to the Credit history on the same page, which is also shown to auditors and office staff on the Customer Credit Account screen. New customers get a next review date automatically, a standard period (set in Credit Control Settings) after the account is opened.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditControlReport">Credit control report</a></h3>
+
+<p>Under Reports, Credit Control Report lists the customers due or overdue for a credit review, the customers over their credit limit (and by how much, including open orders) and the customers on watch or on stop. It can be filtered by sales person and by currency and totals are in the home currency. A sales rep logon only sees the rep's own customers. The aged debtors report also shows each customer's credit status, credit limit and last reminder stage sent.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditControlOrders">Credit control orders</a></h3>
+
+<p>With Check Credit Limits set to <i>Hold order for credit control approval</i> (System Parameters), an order that takes the customer over their limit is accepted but held: it cannot be dispatched or invoiced until credit control approves it here. The same screen lists open orders for customers on stop (to release them if wanted) and open orders for customers on watch.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditControlReminders">Reminder letters</a></h3>
+
+<p>The reminder run (Transactions, Credit Control Reminder Run) is started by credit control, normally weekly. It first shows who would get which letter. Untick any customer to leave them out of this run - for example where a payment has been promised. Then <i>Send the letters</i> emails each letter to the customer's contacts that are flagged to receive statements, in the customer's currency, listing the overdue invoices. Customers with no such contact are listed so the letter can be printed and posted.</p>
+<p>A customer moves up one stage at a time and only while the invoices they were chased for are still unpaid: a customer who pays gets no further letter. The same stage is never sent twice and nothing is sent within the minimum number of days since the last letter. Disputed invoices are not chased. After the final stage, the customer is listed for credit control to decide whether to put them on stop - this is never done automatically. Every letter is recorded against the customer with the invoices it covered and shows in the customer's portal.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditControlCharges">Month end interest and late fees</a></h3>
+
+<p>At the end of a month, Transactions, Month End Interest and Late Fees shows what will be charged to whom. Interest is a twelfth of the annual rate on the balance that is more than the grace days past due and not disputed; the late fee is one flat fee for the month (in the home currency, converted to the customer's currency). Interest is not charged on interest or fees. Customers exempted on their credit file are left out and any customer can be unticked. <i>Confirm and post</i> raises the charges as separate items on the customer's account - shown on the statement as Late Payment Interest and Late Payment Fee - and posts them to the income accounts set in Credit Control Settings. A customer is charged once a month for each kind of charge. A posted charge can be waived with a reason, which credits the customer and reverses the ledger posting.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditControlWriteOffs">Bad debt write offs</a></h3>
+
+<p>Transactions, Bad Debt Write Offs writes off all or part of a customer's balance with a reason. Up to the limit set in Credit Control Settings it posts straight away; a larger write off waits for the Finance Director, who cannot approve a write off they requested. A write off credits the customer's account, debits the bad debt account (any difference on exchange goes to the sales exchange difference account) and, unless unticked, puts the customer on stop. A bad debt later paid by the customer appears as an unallocated receipt on the account; post any reclassification to the ledger with a journal. The same screen reports write offs over a period, with the amount, reason, who requested and who approved them.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditControlSetup">Credit control settings</a></h3>
+
+<p>Maintenance, Credit Control Settings (Finance Director) sets the standard review period, interest rate, late fee, days overdue before a balance is charged, the income and bad debt GL accounts, the write off approval limit, the credit statuses used for normal, watch and stop, and the reminder letters: how many stages there are, how many days overdue triggers each and the wording. A letter can use {customer}, {company}, {currency}, {total}, {invoices} and {date}. Next review dates can also be set here for all customers.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditControlImport">Going live</a></h3>
+
+<p>Maintenance, Load Credit Status from .csv file loads the credit controller's spreadsheet: customer code, status (normal, watch or stop), credit limit and next review date. Tick <i>Check the file only</i> first. Everyone on the old stop list must be loaded as stop. Every change is written to the credit history.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditControlReconcile">Month end agreement with the ledger</a></h3>
+
+<p>Interest, fees, waivers and write offs post to the debtors control account, so the customer balances stay equal to the ledger. Under Utilities, Debtors Control Integrity compares them by period and, for the periods chosen, lists any customer transaction whose ledger posting differs and any posting made directly to the debtors control account (such as a manual journal).</p>
+
+<h3>A note for sales staff</h3>
+
+<p>If a customer is <b>on watch</b> you can still take an order, but you will be warned: talk to the customer about their account first. If a customer is <b>on stop</b> the system will not accept the order from anyone, and goods already ordered will not be dispatched. You cannot change either status or the credit limit - contact credit control. You can see the status, limit, balance, overdue amount and reminders for your own customers on the Customer Credit Account screen.</p>
+
+<div class="floatright">
+    <a class="minitext" href="#top">⬆ Top</a>
+</div>
